<div class="prose-block">

<p>An email is sitting at the top of my inbox, bold and unread, from the university registrar's office. The subject line reads "Name Confirmation Processed."</p>

<p>I open it. "Dear Mia Cole, thank you for confirming your preferred name for academic correspondence. Your records have been updated in our system effective immediately. Course enrollment and transcripts will display your preferred name. Official legal documents will continue to use your legal name on file until a legal name change is processed."</p>

<p>I read it three times. Course enrollment, transcripts, all of it. The professor calling roll in Advanced Statistics will look at the roster, read "Mia Cole," look up expecting a girl, and see a girl, because that's what I am now.</p>

<p>A legal name change is a whole other thing. Court documents and filing fees and standing in front of a judge explaining why Marcus Aiden Cole needs to legally become Mia Something Cole. I don't even have a middle name picked. Do I need a middle name? Did Marcus's carry over, or did the transformation eat that too?</p>

<p>The email treats this like routine paperwork, a preferred name update processed, the same as changing an email signature. As if the distance between Marcus and Mia is administrative rather than existential.</p>

<p>I close the email. I'll think about the legal name change later. I'll think about everything later. Later is a room in my head where I shove the things I can't look at directly, and the door is getting harder to close.</p>

</div>

