<p>The evening settles around me like silt at the bottom of a pond. Quiet, heavy, slow.</p>

<p>I wash the pasta dish and wipe down the counter. These small domestic acts feel louder than they should, the clink of ceramic against steel, the hiss of the faucet, the squeak of the sponge on the plate. I'm filling silence with noise because silence leaves too much room for thinking.</p>

<p>I change into clean panties, white cotton this time, from the three-pack Marlene sold me. They fit the same as the black ones, snug against curves that shouldn't be there but are. I pull the old t-shirt back on and stand in the kitchen doorway.</p>

<p>The apartment is small enough that I can see every room from this spot. Mattress on the floor with its damp patch from this afternoon. Bathroom door open, mirror reflecting a slice of wall. Kitchen counter with the jar of tomato sauce still open. Living area, which is really just a couch and a TV on a milk crate.</p>

<p>This is where I live now. Not Marcus's apartment anymore, mine, Mia's. A girl's apartment that still has a boy's stuff in it, because the girl hasn't had time to redecorate.</p>

<p>I turn on the TV, some reality show about people renovating houses. The noise fills the apartment and pushes the silence back, and I sit on the couch and pull my knees up to my chest and feel the way my thighs press against my breasts, the way everything on this body sits closer together now, more compact, folding inward instead of sprawling outward.</p>

